# Environment variables read by this API — NAMES ONLY, never values.
#
# The deploy workflow applies these to the Container App from GitHub secrets and
# variables, so configuration is reviewable in a pull request and restorable if
# the app is ever recreated. This file is the map: it says, for each name,
# whether GitHub holds it as a secret or a variable.
#
# Each entry is marked one of:
#
#   GitHub secret     credential — create under Settings > Environments > dev >
#                     Secrets. Masked in logs and write-only once saved.
#   GitHub variable   not confidential — create under the Variables tab. Kept
#                     readable on purpose: GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID has to be compared
#                     against the Portal's NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, and a
#                     value masked as *** in the log cannot be compared with
#                     anything. That masking is part of why the two drifted
#                     apart unnoticed until every Google sign-in returned 401.
#   not deployed      the app runs on the default in app/core/config.py. Listed
#                     so the setting is discoverable, not because it is missing.
#
# A name absent from GitHub is SKIPPED by the deploy, never written as empty,
# and named in the run log. Migration can therefore proceed one variable at a
# time without a flag day, and the log states exactly how much is still manual.
#
# An empty value is not the same as an unset one. Several defaults are active:
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID defaults to "" and that REJECTS every token rather than
# skipping the audience check, and PUBLIC_API_PASSWORD defaults to a literal
# committed to this repository. Never create a GitHub secret with a blank value
# to "turn something off".
#
# Regenerated from the Field(...) declarations in app/core/config.py. Compare
# against the live app with:
#
#   az containerapp show -n rivollo-dev-portal-api -g <rg> \
#     --query "properties.template.containers[].env[].name" -o tsv | sort

